'''temporale''' (tem-pō-rä′lē), ''n.'' [ML., neut. of L. ''temporalis'', of the time: see ''temporal''<sup>1</sup>.] That part of the breviary and missal which contains the proper portions of the daily offices, in the order of the ecclesiastical year, beginning with Advent.

'''temporality''' (tem-pō-ral′i-ti), ''n.''; pl. ''temporalities'' (-tiz). [Early mod. E. also ''temporalitie''; < OF. ''temporalite'', F. ''temporalité'' = Sp. ''temporalidad'' = Pg. ''temporalidade'' = It. ''temporalità'', < LL. ''temporalita(t-)s'', temporariness, present custom, fashion, < L. ''temporalis'', of the time: see ''temporal''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' In ''Eng. law'', the state or character of being temporary: opposed to ''perpetuity''.—'''2†.''' The laity.

{{smaller block|Wherwith who so findeth faulte blamed not onelye the clergie but also the ''temporalitie'', which be and haue bene al this while partners in the authoritie of the making and conseruacion of this lawe. {{right|''Sir T. More'', Works, p. 232.}}}}

'''3.''' A secular possession; specifically (in the plural), property and revenues of a religious corporation or an ecclesiastic, held for religious uses: contradistinguished from ''spiritualities'', or matters of which the civil courts have no jurisdiction.

{{smaller block|Many hold ''temporalities'', tithes, and glebes unlawful. {{right|''Rev. S. Ward'', Sermons, p. 75.}}}}

{{smaller block|Having a sufficient fortune of my own, I was careless of ''temporalities.'' {{right|''Goldsmith'', Vicar, ii.}}}}

'''Guardian of the temporalities.''' {{smaller|See ''guardian.''}}

'''temporally''' (tem′pō-ral-i), ''adv.'' With reference to time; specifically, with respect to the present life only.

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|Sinners who are in such a ''temporally'' happy condition owe it not to their sins, but wholly to their luck.
{{right|''South'', Sermons.}}}}

¶
'''temporalness''' (tem′pọ̄-ral-nes), ''n.'' The state or quality of being temporal; worldliness. ''Cotgrave.''

'''temporalty''' (tem′pọ̄-ral-ti), ''n.''; pl. ''temporalties'' (-tiz). [Early mod. E. also ''temporaltie''; < OF. *''temporalte'': see ''temporality''.] 1. The laity; secular persons; secular affairs.

{{smaller block|The prince of Moscouie . . . vsurpeth this autoritie aswell ouer the spiritualtie as the ''temporaltie'': constitutynge what him lysteth of the goods and lyfe of al men.
{{right|''R. Eden'', tr. of Sigismundus Liberus (First Books on [America, ed. Arber, p. 318).}}}}

{{smaller block|If now we attempt to find in Henry's treatment of the ''temporalty'' a reflexion of the principles on which he dealt thus summarily with the spirituality, what do we find?
{{right|''Stubbs'', Medieval and Modern Hist., p. 262.}}}}

2. A secular possession; a temporality.

{{smaller block|The Caliph (who retayned the highest place still in their superstition, although dispoyled of his ''Temporalties'').
{{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 279.}}}}

